<edge source="china_west_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="bond_market_stress::bond_market_liquidity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Spikes in geopolitical tension prompt dealer risk-limit retrenchment and reduce market-making capacity (wider spreads, lower depth) as participants withdraw from corporate and regional sovereign paper, directly compressing bond market liquidity.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">china_west_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">bond_market_stress</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_west_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="global_energy_volatility::reserve_currency_index" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Spikes in geopolitical tension prompt rapid safe‑haven capital flows into major reserve currencies (especially USD), mechanically raising their exchange value and the composite reserve currency index within weeks as traders reprice risk and central banks/sovereign funds shift short‑term allocations.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">china_west_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">global_energy_volatility</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_west_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="global_energy_volatility::market_liquidity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Heightened China‑West geopolitical tension drives surges in physical gold buying and off‑exchange settlement requests while dealers widen spreads and reduce inventory, making it harder to execute very large gold trades (i.e., reducing observable market liquidity).</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">china_west_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">global_energy_volatility</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_west_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="gulf_proxy_campaign::kinetic_attack_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">A spike in China–West geopolitical tension prompts visible Western naval/security deployments, sanctions and interdiction operations in the Gulf/Red Sea that Iran-aligned actors interpret as direct threats and respond with an uptick in missile/drone/ship-based strikes on shipping and energy infrastructure.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">china_west_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">gulf_proxy_campaign</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_west_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="gulf_proxy_campaign::military_response_intens" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising rivalry accelerates intelligence-sharing, weapons transfers and joint operations between Western and Gulf security forces, enabling larger-scale and higher-tempo kinetic countermeasures when proxies strike.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">china_west_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">gulf_proxy_campaign</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_west_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="gulf_proxy_campaign::airline_cancellation_rat" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Higher China–West strategic tensions raise the assessed risk to commercial routes (due to increased maritime/airspace incidents and insurance costs), prompting carriers and insurers to suspend or reroute flights through Gulf/Red Sea airspace and increase cancellations.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">china_west_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">gulf_proxy_campaign</data>
</edge>
<edge source="china_west_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="russia_ukraine_war::alliance_cohesion_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A spike in China–West strategic friction prompts coordinated diplomatic messaging and joint security signaling (e.g., joint statements, coordinated sanctions posture) among Western partners, increasing political and operational alignment relevant to Ukraine policy in the near term.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
